{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_7","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '1,260p' /Users/smkzw/.cc-switch/skills/clinical-research/clinical-competitive-intelligence/SKILL.md && sed -n '1,220p' .trellis/workflow.md\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_7","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '1,260p' /Users/smkzw/.cc-switch/skills/clinical-research/clinical-competitive-intelligence/SKILL.md && sed -n '1,220p' .trellis/workflow.md\"","aggregated_output":"---\nname: clinical-competitive-intelligence\ndescription: 'Generate structured competitive intelligence reports for therapeutic area landscape analysis. Covers: competitor drug identification from registries/literature/regulatory sources, clinical trial design comparison, efficacy/safety data extraction, regulatory status tracking, drug lifecycle analysis, and clinical development strategy recommendations. Produces single-file interactive HTML reports with embedded SVG charts, structured SQLite databases, and evidence registries.\n\n  '\nmetadata:\n  triggers:\n  - competitive intelligence report\n  - 竞品调研\n  - 竞争格局分析\n  - clinical landscape analysis\n  - drug pipeline research\n  - 临床开发策略\n  - therapeutic area competitive analysis\n  - CFB/CFD/complement inhibitor landscape\n  - 研发进度调研\n  - ICH E1 safety database analysis\n  - 安全性数据库分析\n  - drug development safety database\n  - multi-indication development strategy\n  - 多适应症开发策略\n  - 安全数据库1500例\n---\n\n# Clinical Competitive Intelligence Report Generation\n\n## When to Use\n\nWhen the user requests a competitive landscape analysis, drug pipeline research, or clinical\ndevelopment strategy report for any therapeutic area. The workflow produces:\n\n1. **Structured SQLite database** with products, trials, efficacy, safety, regulatory, dev history\n2. **Evidence registry CSV** tracing every data point to its source\n3. **Single-file interactive HTML report** with SVG charts, filterable tables, and linked evidence\n\n## Workflow Overview\n\n### Phase 0: Workspace Initialization\n\n1. Create standardized directory structure:\n```\n<project_root>/\n├── 00_admin/          # task_state.json, master_plan, evidence_registry, QC files\n├── 01_raw/            # Downloaded raw files by source type\n│   ├── clinicaltrials_gov/\n│   ├── regulatory_fda/\n│   ├── regulatory_ema/\n│   ├── regulatory_nmpa_cde/\n│   ├── pubmed_pmc_journals/\n│   ├── company_news/\n│   ├── conferences/\n│   └── wechat_and_cn_media/\n├── 02_documents/      # Normalized documents (protocols, SAPs, publications, labels)\n├── 03_extracted/      # SQLite DB + CSV exports\n├── 04_figures_svg/    # SVG figures\n├── 05_report/         # Final HTML report\n├── 06_zotero_import/  # RIS/BibTeX + attachments\n└── 07_visual_review/  # Screenshots for QA\n```\n\n2. Scan existing files, build index with MD5 hashes, create `existing_files_index.csv`\n3. Initialize SQLite schema (see `references/sqlite-schema.md`)\n4. Create `task_state.json` tracking progress per phase\n\n### Phase 1: Competitor Drug & Trial Identification\n\n**Search ClinicalTrials.gov** with multiple query strategies:\n- Disease name + synonyms (e.g., \"paroxysmal nocturnal hemoglobinuria\", \"PNH\", \"hemoglobinuria, paroxysmal\")\n- Target names (e.g., C5, C3, CFB, CFD, MASP-3, complement)\n- Known drug names, brand names, development codes\n- Company/sponsor names\n\n**Key pitfall**: Don't rely only on the Condition field. Some studies use title/description\nfor PNH indication. Search broadly, then filter with explicit inclusion/exclusion criteria.\n\n**For each NCT record**, extract: study design, phase, status, arms, sample size, eligibility\ncriteria (Hb threshold, LDH threshold, transfusion requirement, prior treatment, washout),\nprimary/secondary endpoints, AESI, extension/rollover design.\n\n**Cross-reference** with regional registries (chinadrugtrials.org.cn, EU CTR) where accessible.\nMark \"not found\" when a registry is inaccessible — never fabricate CTR/regional IDs.\n\n### Phase 2: Evidence Collection (Parallel Subagents)\n\nUse `delegate_task` with 3-5 parallel subagents for independent research streams:\n- **Agent A**: Registry + trial design extraction\n- **Agent B**: Protocol/SAP PDF search and download\n- **Agent C**: Publication search (PubMed, PMC, conference abstracts)\n- **Agent D**: Regulatory documents (FDA Drugs@FDA, EMA EPAR, NMPA/CDE)\n- **Agent E**: Company sources, news, Chinese media\n\nEach subagent returns: structured findings with URLs, dates, source reliability level.\nMain agent merges results — subagents write to temp files, main agent consolidates.\n\n**Evidence hierarchy**: See `references/evidence-hierarchy.md`\n\n### Phase 3: Data Extraction & Database Population\n\nExtract from collected sources into SQLite tables:\n- `products`: Drug master data (target, mechanism, modality, route, regulatory status)\n- `trials`: Trial design (NCT, phase, arms, eligibility, endpoints)\n- `efficacy`: Efficacy results by trial/arm/endpoint (long format)\n- `safety`: Safety events by trial/arm/event (long format)\n- `baseline`: Baseline characteristics (demographics, disease, labs)\n- `regulatory_status`: FDA/EMA/NMPA approval status\n- `development_history`: Full lifecycle timeline\n- `publications`: Paper/trial/publication mapping\n\n**Key pitfall**: ClinicalTrials.gov auto-extracted results often have empty `drug_name` fields\nin efficacy/safety tables. Build a `trial_uid → drug_name` mapping from the trials table\nor trial_arms table before aggregating.\n\n### Phase 4: HTML Report Generation\n\nBuild single-file interactive HTML. See `references/html-report-template.md` for full template.\n\n**Structure**: Cover → Chapter 1 (Trial Design) → Chapter 2 (Results) → Chapter 3 (Lifecycle) → Chapter 4 (Strategy) → Appendix\n\n**Critical pattern**: Generate HTML in chunks using `execute_code` + `write_file` to avoid\ncontext overflow. Write CSS/head first, then each chapter, then JS, then assemble.\n\n**Charts**: All charts must be inline SVG (no Canvas, no external CDN, no bitmap charts).\nGenerate SVG strings in Python, embed directly in HTML.\n\n**Tables**: Use `overflow-x:auto` wrapper for wide tables. Filter bars with JS for\ninteractive filtering. Sticky headers.\n\n**Color scheme**: Primary accent `#FF9900`, info `#2196F3`, success `#4CAF50`,\ndanger `#F44336`. Medical/professional aesthetic — dense information, minimal decoration.\n\n### Phase 5: Quality Assurance\n\n1. Open in browser, verify at 1440px and 1920px\n2. Check: navigation works, SVGs render, tables scroll, links clickable, no text overflow\n3. Independent medical review: evidence gaps, misleading comparisons, unit inconsistencies\n4. Save screenshots to `07_visual_review/`\n5. Fix issues, regenerate, version increment\n\n## File Naming Conventions\n\n**Protocols**: `【靶点】【药物】【申办方】【分期】【年份】【关键III期与否】【NCT编号】【监管状态】研究方案.pdf`\n**Publications**: `【靶点】【药物】【申办方】【分期】【发表年份】【NCT编号】【监管状态】结果.pdf`\n**FDA docs**: `【靶点】【药物】【申办方】【适应症】-FDA说明书.pdf`\n\nSanitize illegal characters: `/ : ? * \" < > |` → replace with `_`\n\n## Pitfalls\n\n1. **ClinicalTrials.gov drug_name mapping**: Efficacy/safety result tables extracted from CT.gov\n   often have empty drug_name. Always build NCT→drug mapping from trials/trial_arms tables first.\n\n2. **Cross-trial comparison**: Different trials use different timepoints (12/16/24/26 weeks),\n   different Hb thresholds, different LDH normalization criteria. Always footnote these\n   differences — never present a bare comparison table without context.\n\n3. **Hb unit conversion**: Record both original unit and g/L. 1 g/dL = 10 g/L.\n\n4. **Small sample caution**: Phase 1/2 trials with n<30 need explicit sample size annotation\n   on all charts and tables. Don't rank small-n results alongside large-n Phase 3 data.\n\n5. **Chinese media verification**: WeChat/公众号 articles (except 丁香园 Insight, 医药魔方,\n   药智网) must be verified against official/primary sources before entering the database.\n\n6. **HTML chunk assembly**: When generating large HTML, write to temp files per section, then\n   assemble. Don't try to build 100KB+ HTML in a single string variable — context overflow risk.\n\n7. **SVG text rotation**: Drug name labels on matrix charts need `transform=\"rotate(-45,x,y)\"`\n   to avoid overlap. Test with actual drug name lengths.\n\n8. **Regulatory status disambiguation**: Same drug may have multiple application numbers,\n   indications, and label versions. Filter to the target indication before recording status.\n\n9. **Version numbering**: Check existing files in the project directory before assigning version.\n   Auto-increment if same-date same-version exists. Run `ls -la <dir>/*V0.*.html` before writing\n   to avoid overwriting a prior version generated in a different session.\n\n10. **ICH E1 exposure**: Don't cite ICH E1 from memory. See `references/ich-e1a-guideline-full-text.md`\n    for exact wording and `references/ich-e1-safety-database-analysis.md` for the full regulatory\n    analysis. Critical nuance: the 1500-patient threshold is an \"anticipated... about\" number, not\n    a hard requirement. The guideline itself lists exceptions (§7) including §7(e) — \"a smaller\n    number of patients may be acceptable... where the intended treatment population is small\" —\n    which is the primary basis for rare disease approvals with <200 patients. Always distinguish\n    between common disease targets (where 1500+ is expected) and rare disease drugs (where §7(e)\n    applies and smaller databases are explicitly allowed).\n\n11. **HTML report code audit checklist** (V0.8 lesson — 3 bugs found): Before delivering a large\n    single-file HTML report, run these automated checks via `grep` + browser console:\n    - **Undefined JS functions**: Search for function names in `onclick`/`onchange` handlers that\n      are never defined in `<script>`. E.g. `filterTable` called in sidebar but missing from JS.\n    - **Empty container divs**: Check `getElementById` targets — any `<div id=\"table-X\"></div>` or\n      `<div id=\"chart-X\"></div>` that `renderAllCharts()` never populates leaves a blank section.\n    - **Drug name typos**: `grep` for known drug names and check for near-misses (e.g. raculizumab\n      vs ravulizumab). Drug names appear in many contexts and typos propagate silently.\n    - **Browser console validation**: After opening the report, run in console:\n      `JSON.stringify({pages: [...document.querySelectorAll('.page')].map(p=>p.id+':'+(p.classList.contains('active')?'visible':'hidden')), charts: [...document.querySelectorAll('[id^=chart-]')].map(e=>e.id+':'+(e.innerHTML.length>10?'OK':'EMPTY')), tables: [...document.querySelectorAll('[id^=table-]')].map(e=>e.id+':'+(e.querySelector('table')?'OK':'MISSING'))})` \n      to verify all containers rendered.\n\n12. **Fact verification for recently-approved drugs**: When reviewing a report's regulatory claims\n    for drugs with very recent approval dates (within days), cross-verify against NMPA/FDA/EMA\n    official announcements via web search. Approval dates reported in Chinese media may differ\n    from official announcement dates by 1-2 days. Use the official date, not media date.\n\n13. **ICH E1 safety database (multi-indication drugs)**: See `references/ich-e1-safety-database-analysis.md`\n    for the complete workflow on how to research and present how a drug achieves the ICH E1\n    1500-patient safety database threshold through multi-indication development. Key technique:\n    extract total exposed from FDA label Section 6.1 first, then reconstruct per-indication\n    contribution from ClinicalTrials.gov v2 API, then build cumulative timeline per regulatory\n    milestone. See reference for common strategies (parallel vs sequential vs basket).\n# Development Workflow\n\n---\n\n## Core Principles\n\n1. **Plan before code** — figure out what to do before you start\n2. **Specs injected, not remembered** — guidelines are injected via hook/skill, not recalled from memory\n3. **Persist everything** — research, decisions, and lessons all go to files; conversations get compacted, files don't\n4. **Incremental development** — one task at a time\n5. **Capture learnings** — after each task, review and write new knowledge back to spec\n\n---\n\n## Trellis System\n\n### Developer Identity\n\nOn first use, initialize your identity:\n\n```bash\npython3 ./.trellis/scripts/init_developer.py <your-name>\n```\n\nCreates `.trellis/.developer` (gitignored) + `.trellis/workspace/<your-name>/`.\n\n### Spec System\n\n`.trellis/spec/` holds coding guidelines organized by package and layer.\n\n- `.trellis/spec/<package>/<layer>/index.md` — entry point with **Pre-Development Checklist** + **Quality Check**. Actual guidelines live in the `.md` files it points to.\n- `.trellis/spec/guides/index.md` — cross-package thinking guides.\n\n```bash\npython3 ./.trellis/scripts/get_context.py --mode packages   # list packages / layers\n```\n\n**When to update spec**: new pattern/convention found · bug-fix prevention to codify · new technical decision.\n\n### Task System\n\nEvery task has its own directory under `.trellis/tasks/{MM-DD-name}/` holding `task.json`, `prd.md`, optional `design.md`, optional `implement.md`, optional `research/`, and context manifests (`implement.jsonl`, `check.jsonl`) for sub-agent-capable platforms.\n\n```bash\n# Task lifecycle\npython3 ./.trellis/scripts/task.py create \"<title>\" [--slug <name>] [--parent <dir>]\npython3 ./.trellis/scripts/task.py start <name>          # set active task (session-scoped when available)\npython3 ./.trellis/scripts/task.py current --source      # show active task and source\npython3 ./.trellis/scripts/task.py finish                # clear active task (triggers after_finish hooks)\npython3 ./.trellis/scripts/task.py archive <name>        # move to archive/{year-month}/\npython3 ./.trellis/scripts/task.py list [--mine] [--status <s>]\npython3 ./.trellis/scripts/task.py list-archive\n\n# Code-spec context (injected into implement/check agents via JSONL).\n# `implement.jsonl` / `check.jsonl` are seeded on `task create` for sub-agent-capable\n# platforms; the AI curates real spec + research entries during planning when needed.\npython3 ./.trellis/scripts/task.py add-context <name> <action> <file> <reason>\npython3 ./.trellis/scripts/task.py list-context <name> [action]\npython3 ./.trellis/scripts/task.py validate <name>\n\n# Task metadata\npython3 ./.trellis/scripts/task.py set-branch <name> <branch>\npython3 ./.trellis/scripts/task.py set-base-branch <name> <branch>    # PR target\npython3 ./.trellis/scripts/task.py set-scope <name> <scope>\n\n# Hierarchy (parent/child)\npython3 ./.trellis/scripts/task.py add-subtask <parent> <child>\npython3 ./.trellis/scripts/task.py remove-subtask <parent> <child>\n\n# PR creation\npython3 ./.trellis/scripts/task.py create-pr [name] [--dry-run]\n```\n\n> Run `python3 ./.trellis/scripts/task.py --help` to see the authoritative, up-to-date list.\n\n**Current-task mechanism**: `task.py create` creates the task directory and (when session identity is available) auto-sets the per-session active-task pointer so the planning breadcrumb fires immediately. `task.py start` writes the same pointer (idempotent if already set) and flips `task.json.status` from `planning` to `in_progress`. State is stored under `.trellis/.runtime/sessions/`. If no context key is available from hook input, `TRELLIS_CONTEXT_ID`, or a platform-native session environment variable, there is no active task and `task.py start` fails with a session identity hint. `task.py finish` deletes the current session file (status unchanged). `task.py archive <task>` writes `status=completed`, moves the directory to `archive/`, and deletes any runtime session files that still point at the archived task.\n\n### Workspace System\n\nRecords every AI session for cross-session tracking under `.trellis/workspace/<developer>/`.\n\n- `journal-N.md` — session log. **Max 2000 lines per file**; a new `journal-(N+1).md` is auto-created when exceeded.\n- `index.md` — personal index (total sessions, last active).\n\n```bash\npython3 ./.trellis/scripts/add_session.py --title \"Title\" --commit \"hash\" --summary \"Summary\"\n```\n\n### Context Script\n\n```bash\npython3 ./.trellis/scripts/get_context.py                            # full session runtime\npython3 ./.trellis/scripts/get_context.py --mode packages            # available packages + spec layers\npython3 ./.trellis/scripts/get_context.py --mode phase --step <X.Y>  # detailed guide for a workflow step\n```\n\n---\n\n<!--\n  WORKFLOW-STATE BREADCRUMB CONTRACT (read this before editing the tag blocks below)\n\n  The [workflow-state:STATUS] blocks embedded in the ## Phase Index section\n  below are the SINGLE source of truth for the per-turn `<workflow-state>`\n  breadcrumb that every supported AI platform's UserPromptSubmit hook\n  reads. inject-workflow-state.py (Python platforms) and\n  inject-workflow-state.js (OpenCode plugin) only parse them — there is no\n  fallback dict baked into the scripts after v0.5.0-rc.0.\n\n  STATUS charset: [A-Za-z0-9_-]+. When the hook can't find a tag, it\n  degrades to a generic \"Refer to workflow.md for current step.\" line —\n  intentionally visible so users notice and fix a broken workflow.md.\n\n  INVARIANT (test/regression.test.ts):\n    Every workflow-walkthrough step marked `[required · once]` must have a\n    matching enforcement line in its phase's [workflow-state:*] block. The\n    breadcrumb is the only per-turn channel; if a mandatory step isn't\n    mentioned there, the AI silently skips it (Phase 1 planning gate\n    skip and Phase 3.4 commit skip both manifested via this gap).\n\n  TAG ↔ PHASE scoping:\n    [workflow-state:no_task]      → no active task; before Phase 1\n    [workflow-state:planning]     → all of Phase 1 (status='planning')\n    [workflow-state:planning-inline] → Codex inline variant of Phase 1\n    [workflow-state:in_progress]  → Phase 2 + Phase 3.2-3.4\n                                    (status stays 'in_progress' from\n                                    task.py start until task.py archive)\n    [workflow-state:in_progress-inline] → Codex inline variant of Phase 2/3\n    [workflow-state:completed]    → currently DEAD: cmd_archive flips\n                                    status and moves the dir in the same\n                                    call, so the resolver loses the\n                                    pointer (block kept for a future\n                                    explicit in_progress→completed\n                                    transition)\n\n  Editing checklist:\n    - When you change a [workflow-state:STATUS] block, also check the\n      matching phase's `[required · once]` walkthrough steps for sync\n    - Run `trellis update` after editing to push the new bodies to\n      downstream user projects (block-level managed replacement)\n    - Full runtime contract:\n      .trellis/spec/cli/backend/workflow-state-contract.md\n-->\n\n## Phase Index\n\n```\nPhase 1: Plan    → classify, get task-creation consent, then write planning artifacts\nPhase 2: Execute → implement only after task status is in_progress\nPhase 3: Finish  → verify, update spec, commit, and wrap up\n```\n\n### Request Triage\n\n- Simple conversation or small task: ask only whether this turn should create a Trellis task. If the user says no, skip Trellis for this session.\n- Complex task: ask whether you may create a Trellis task and enter planning. If the user says no, do not do broad inline implementation; explain, clarify scope, or suggest a smaller split.\n- User approval to create a task is not approval to start implementation. Planning still happens first.\n\n### Planning Artifacts\n\n- `prd.md` — requirements, constraints, and acceptance criteria. Do not put technical design or execution checklists here.\n- `design.md` — technical design for complex tasks: boundaries, contracts, data flow, tradeoffs, compatibility, rollout / rollback shape.\n- `implement.md` — execution plan for complex tasks: ordered checklist, validation commands, review gates, and rollback points.\n- `implement.jsonl` / `check.jsonl` — spec and research manifests for sub-agent context. They do not replace `implement.md`.\n- Lightweight tasks may be PRD-only. Complex tasks must have `prd.md`, `design.md`, and `implement.md` before `task.py start`.\n\n### Parent / Child Task Trees\n\nUse a parent task when one user request contains several independently verifiable deliverables. The parent task owns the source requirement set, the task map, cross-child acceptance criteria, and final integration review; it normally should not be the implementation target unless it also has direct work.\n\nUse child tasks for deliverables that can be planned, implemented, checked, and archived independently. Parent/child structure is not a dependency system: if one child must wait for another, write that ordering in the child `prd.md` / `implement.md` and keep each child's acceptance criteria testable.\n\nCreate new children with `task.py create \"<title>\" --slug <name> --parent <parent-dir>`. Link existing tasks with `task.py add-subtask <parent> <child>`, and unlink mistakes with `task.py remove-subtask <parent> <child>`.\n\n<!-- Per-turn breadcrumb: shown when there is no active task (before Phase 1) -->\n\n[workflow-state:no_task]\nNo active task. First classify the current turn and ask for task-creation consent before creating any Trellis task.\nSimple conversation / small task: ask only whether this turn should create a Trellis task. If the user says no, skip Trellis for this session.\nComplex task: ask the user if you can create a Trellis task and enter the planning phase. If the user says no, explain, clarify scope, or suggest a smaller split.\n[/workflow-state:no_task]\n\n### Phase 1: Plan\n- 1.0 Create task `[required · once]` (only after task-creation consent)\n- 1.1 Requirement exploration `[required · repeatable]` (`prd.md`; complex tasks also need `design.md` + `implement.md`)\n- 1.2 Research `[optional · repeatable]`\n- 1.3 Configure context `[required · once]` — Claude Code, Cursor, OpenCode, Codex, Kiro, Gemini, Qoder, CodeBuddy, Copilot, Droid, Pi, Oh My Pi, ZCode, Snow, Reasonix, Grok, Kimi Code (sub-agent-dispatch platforms only; inline platforms skip)\n- 1.4 Activate task `[required · once]` (review gate, then `task.py start`; status → in_progress)\n- 1.5 Completion criteria\n\n<!-- Per-turn breadcrumb: shown throughout Phase 1 (status='planning') -->\n\n[workflow-state:planning]\nLoad `trellis-brainstorm`; stay in planning.\nLightweight: `prd.md` can be enough. Complex: finish `prd.md`, `design.md`, and `implement.md`; ask for review before `task.py start`.\nMulti-deliverable scope: consider a parent task plus independently verifiable child tasks; dependencies must be written in child artifacts, not implied by tree position.\nSub-agent mode: curate `implement.jsonl` and `check.jsonl` as spec/research manifests before start.\n[/workflow-state:planning]\n\n<!-- Per-turn breadcrumb: shown throughout Phase 1 when codex.dispatch_mode=inline.\n     Codex-only opt-in alternate to [workflow-state:planning]. The main agent\n     edits code directly in Phase 2, so jsonl curation is skipped —\n     the inline workflow loads `trellis-before-dev` instead of injecting JSONL\n     into a sub-agent. -->\n\n[workflow-state:planning-inline]\nLoad `trellis-brainstorm`; stay in planning.\nLightweight: `prd.md` can be enough. Complex: finish `prd.md`, `design.md`, and `implement.md`; ask for review before `task.py start`.\nMulti-deliverable scope: consider a parent task plus independently verifiable child tasks; dependencies must be written in child artifacts, not implied by tree position.\nInline mode: skip jsonl curation; Phase 2 reads artifacts/specs via `trellis-before-dev`.\n[/workflow-state:planning-inline]\n\n### Phase 2: Execute\n- 2.1 Implement `[required · repeatable]`\n- 2.2 Quality check `[required · repeatable]`\n- 2.3 Rollback `[on demand]`\n\n<!-- Per-turn breadcrumb: shown while status='in_progress'.\n     Scope: all of Phase 2 + Phase 3.2-3.4 (status stays 'in_progress' from\n     task.py start until task.py archive; only archive flips it). The body\n     therefore must cover every required step from implementation through\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
